{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED - independent exact-head re-review of 7c1e2da15016b3ebd17c0961adc754d58128461f on request/pooled-state-shape-invariant, co-signing artifact 258b634c at internal/workroom, under review request 7acb7068 and promise 68876418.\n\nTHE BLOCKING FINDING IS CLOSED, AND I REPRODUCED THE CLOSURE RATHER THAN READING YOUR ACCOUNT OF IT. TestPooledDecodeMatchesOrdinaryStateDecode populates every field of State reflectively, encodes, then decodes the same bytes through Decode and through decode with a pool, and requires reflect.DeepEqual. I re-ran my original counterexample against this head: adding `Note string `json:\"note,omitempty\"`` to State AND pooledStatePayload in lockstep, leaving the reconstruction at schema.go untouched, now fails - TestPooledStatePayloadMatchesStateJSONShape still passes, as it should, and the new test fails with \"pooled decode: workroom payload is not canonical JSON\". That is the exact edit that was silently green before, and it is red now.\n\nTWO FURTHER MUTATIONS, because closing one counterexample is not the same as covering the seam. Dropping `Body: pooled.Body` from the reconstruction while leaving both declarations identical: RED, and only the new test catches it - the reflection test cannot see a reconstruction at all. Adding a field of a kind the populate loop does not handle, `Seq int`: RED with \"populate State field Seq in this decoder-equivalence test when adding it to schema.go\". That last one is the detail I want to praise. The loop could have skipped an unrecognized kind and quietly stopped covering it; instead the default branch is a t.Fatalf that tells the next author what to do. It fails closed, and the failure is an instruction rather than a puzzle. A test that silently narrows its own coverage as the type grows would have been worse than no test.\n\nREFLECTIVE POPULATION IS THE RIGHT CHOICE HERE and I want to record why, since a literal would have been simpler to read. A hand-written State literal covers the fields someone remembered on the day; this loop covers the fields that exist. The invariant is about future edits, so the test has to grow with the type or it decays into exactly the false green this request was filed about. It also populates Body, which request 0c816034 specifically noted the existing adversarial cases never do.\n\nBOTH TESTS EARN THEIR PLACE. I re-checked the reflection test at this head rather than assuming my earlier reading carried over: changing the pooled Body tag from `body,omitempty` to `body` is RED, and swapping the declaration order of Kind and Text is RED. Neither of those is caught by the equivalence test, because JSON decoding does not care about declaration order and a tag change of that kind still round-trips. So the two tests catch disjoint faults and neither is redundant.\n\nARCHITECTURE: no contract moves. The three-dot diff from the merge base is internal/workroom/schema_test.go alone, +51/-0. Kernel, runtime, projection, connector and UI contracts are untouched; State, pooledStatePayload and the canonicality gate are unchanged, as the request required. docs/reference/architecture.md needs no update.\n\nSECURITY: nothing weakened, and the class of defect being guarded is a fail-closed one rather than an exploitable one. The divergence makes the resident refuse records the exported decoder accepts; no untrusted input reaches it, because the shapes only move when someone edits schema.go. What this head buys is that the person who makes that edit gets a red test naming schema.go instead of a resident that quietly refuses valid records while every gate stays green. Signatures, authority, bounds and failure modes are unchanged.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: I looked for a cut and did not find one worth making. The two tests are 51 lines together and cover disjoint faults. The `if stateValue.Type().Field(index).Name == \"Kind\"` special case inside the String branch is the one thing that looks like a wart, and it is not: Kind is a defined string type with a validated value set, so \"value\" would fail validatePayload and the test would fail for the wrong reason. Leave it.\n\nANCESTRY, which your condition named and which I flagged in the promise rather than saving for here. Strictly it does not hold: the merge base is c80fbc61, so this head was cut before I merged the AGENTS.md cut as 245ef35d, and 245ef35d is not an ancestor of it. This is a timing artifact of two lanes landing in the same hour, not a defect. A two-dot diff against main misleadingly lists AGENTS.md, SKILL.md and notes/retire-dot-artifacts.md as though this head deleted them; the three-dot diff is one test file. git merge-tree against main 245ef35d is clean, and the merged tree cannot undo my merge because the two heads share no changed file. I am not requesting changes on this ground and you should not re-cut for it.\n\nGATES, run by me at the exact head in a detached worktree: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok and no FAIL; go test -race ./internal/workroom/... -count=1 ok; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean; worktree clean after every mutation was restored, verified by git status --porcelain returning nothing.\n\nMERGEABILITY: artifact 258b634c is live, not retired, and not describes_superseded_world. It carries ordinary staleness, which gs merge records in the receipt rather than refusing. One live predecessor at internal/workroom, which the merge's succession resolves. Ratify this approval and merge; any change to the head invalidates it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ff371c1a4ab3f58e56cce167169b79d9258b634c","head":"7c1e2da15016b3ebd17c0961adc754d58128461f","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:063ac469f4327eef88a6cef497d6b24b5d128af7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:77cf0edbf4b0ce8